---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Eyebrow from '../components/Eyebrow.astro';
import FeatureGrid from '../components/FeatureGrid.astro';
import FeatureItem from '../components/FeatureItem.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import portrait from '../assets/brijesh.jpg';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="About Brijesh Bhakta, a software engineer with 10 years of experience across backend, web, and mobile development."
  image={portrait.src}
>
  <PageHeader eyebrow="About" title="Wide perspective. Careful execution." />
  <section
    class="grid grid-cols-[minmax(240px,0.8fr)_2fr] items-start gap-[clamp(2.5rem,7vw,8rem)] border-b border-rule py-[clamp(5rem,10vw,10rem)] max-mobile:grid-cols-1"
  >
    <img
      class="aspect-[1/1.08] w-full border-b-8 border-accent object-cover grayscale"
      src={portrait.src}
      width={portrait.width}
      height={portrait.height}
      alt="Brijesh Bhakta"
    />
    <div
      class="max-w-[770px] [&>p:first-of-type]:font-serif [&>p:first-of-type]:text-[clamp(1.8rem,3.5vw,3.2rem)] [&>p:first-of-type]:leading-[1.2]"
    >
      <p>
        I’m Brijesh, a software engineer who has spent the last decade working across the layers that make a product useful.
      </p>
      <p>
        My work spans backend systems, complex web applications, and production mobile products. I’ve worked across identity, payments, banking, lending, marketplaces, workplace tools, and real-time systems. That range helps me follow a problem across the product, from the interface and application state through APIs, data, integrations, and the systems behind them.
      </p>
      <p>
        I tend to work across those boundaries rather than within one part of the stack. Alongside building software, I’ve led developers, helped shape technical approaches, and worked through the trade-offs that come with taking products from an idea to something people depend on.
      </p>
      <p>
        I graduated in Computer Science Engineering from Uka Tarsadia University in 2016. The technologies have changed considerably since then, but the way I approach the work has stayed fairly consistent: understand the system, make the trade-offs clear, and leave the code easier to understand.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="border-b border-rule py-[clamp(5rem,10vw,10rem)]">
    <Eyebrow>Working approach</Eyebrow>
    <FeatureGrid>
      <FeatureItem title="Understand the whole">
        <p>
          Good technical choices start with the user, the constraints, and the surrounding system, not with a preferred technology.
        </p>
      </FeatureItem>
      <FeatureItem title="Make complexity legible">
        <p>
          Clear boundaries, direct communication, and maintainable code make products easier to operate and evolve.
        </p>
      </FeatureItem>
      <FeatureItem title="Keep learning practical">
        <p>
          New technology matters when it improves the product, the engineering experience, or the reliability of the system, not simply because it is new.
        </p>
      </FeatureItem>
    </FeatureGrid>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
